Interpretation of Confidence Intervals:
The 99% confidence interval using the t-distribution represents the range within which the true population mean is expected to lie with 99% confidence, based on the sample data. Since the population standard deviation is unknown, the t-distribution provides a more reliable estimate by accounting for sample variability. Similarly, the 99% confidence interval using the z-distribution assumes a known population standard deviation and provides a slightly narrower interval. This interval also indicates the range in which the true population mean is likely to fall with 99% confidence. Overall, both intervals provide an estimate of the population mean, with the t-interval being more appropriate when the sample size is small and population variance is unknown.

Business Conclusion:
Based on the hypothesis testing results, the calculated Z value was compared with the critical value at the chosen significance level. Since the test statistic falls in the rejection region, the null hypothesis is rejected. This indicates that there is sufficient statistical evidence to conclude that the observed sample mean significantly differs from the assumed population mean. From a business perspective, this suggests that the current process or system is not performing as expected and may require further investigation or corrective actions.